```text
Before SSA:                      After SSA:

x = 1                            x1 = 1
if (cond) {                      if (cond) {
    x = 2                            x2 = 2
}                                 }
y = x + 1                        x3 = phi(x1, x2)
                                  y = x3 + 1
```

`x` was assigned twice across two different paths, so which value reaches the `y = x + 1` line depends on whether the branch was taken. SSA can't just pick one — it introduces a **phi node**: `x3 = phi(x1, x2)` means "x3 is x1 if control came from before the if, or x2 if control came from inside it." A phi node is purely a control-flow-merge marker; it's not a runtime operation a CPU executes — it exists to keep the single-assignment property intact exactly at the point where two different definitions could reach the same use.
